1.2 Lesson

1.2.1 Definition
An exponent indicates how many times you should multiply something by itself. For example, \[
a^3 = a \times a \times a \text{.}
\]


1.2.2 Properties
If you multiply two terms with the same base, their exponents add: \[
\begin{align*}
a^3 a^2 &= (a \times a \times a)(a \times a) \\
&= a \times a \times a \times a \times a \\
&= a^5 \\
&= a^{3+2} \text{.}
\end{align*}
\] In the example above, \(a\) is the base. For the first term, \(a^3\), the exponent is \(3\); for the second term, \(a^2\), the exponent is \(2\).
If you divide two terms with the same base, the exponents subtract: \[
\begin{align*}
\frac{a^3}{a^2} &= \frac{a \times a \times a}{a \times a} \\
&= a \\
&= a^1 \\
&= a^{3 - 2} \text{.}
\end{align*}
\] Look at what is implied by this fact: \[
\begin{align*}
a^{-3} &= a^{2-5} \\
&= \frac{a^2}{a^5} \\
&= \frac{a \times a}{a\times a \times a \times a \times a} \\
&= \frac{1}{a\times a\times a} \\
&= \frac{1}{a^3} \text{.} \\
\end{align*}
\]
In general, \[
a^{-b} = \frac{1}{a^b} \text{.}
\]
The next property is one that I see a lot of students get confused about. It says that \[
\left(a^b\right)^c = a^{bc} \text{.}
\] This is easier to see with an example: \[
\begin{align*}
\left(a^3\right)^2 &= a^3 \times a^3 \\
&= (a\times a\times a) \times (a \times a \times a) \\
&= a\times a\times a \times a \times a \times a \\
&= a^6 \\
&= a^{3\times 2} \text{.}
\end{align*}
\]


1.2.3 A Special Base
A very common base in statistics is the special number \(e\), which is approximately equal to 2.71828. It’s like \(\pi\) in that it is really useful in mathematics and can’t be represented as a ratio of two whole numbers. We will see \(e\) over and over again in this class. Sometimes we use the notation \(\exp(b)\) instead of \(e^b\) to make the exponent easier to read. For example, we will see things like this quite a bit: \[
e^{a+bx} = \exp(a+bx) \text{.}
\]


1.2.4 Special Exponents
Anything to the zeroth power is equal to 1: \[
a^0 = 1 \text{.}
\]
Anything to the power of 1 is equal to itself: \[
a^1 = a \text{.}
\]

2.2 Lesson

2.2.1 Relationship to Exponentiation
The logarithm is the opposite of the exponent. In general, \[
\log_a c = b
\] is exactly the same thing as \[
a^b = c \text{.}
\] The expression \(\log_a c\) answers the question, “\(a\) to what power equals \(c\)?”
This means that taking a logarithm “undoes” or “reverses” exponentiation. If we start with \(a^b = c\) and take \(\log_a\) of both sides, we get \[
\begin{align*}
a^b &= c \\
\log_a a^b &= \log_a c \\
b &= \log_a c \text{.}
\end{align*}
\] Why is this true? The expression \(\log_a a^b\) is asking, “\(a\) to what power equals \(a^b\)?” The answer to that question is \(b\).
It’s also true that exponentiation undoes taking a logarithm. The expression \(\log_a c\) is asking the question, “\(a\) to what power equals \(c\)?” Whatever that power is, if we raise \(a\) to it, we’ll get \(c\). So, \[
a^{\log_a c} = c \text{.}
\] This might be more clear with an example. Consider \[
10^{\log_{10} 2} \text{.}
\] I don’t know what \(\log_{10} 2\) is. I know it’s the value that we have to raise 10 to in order to get 2, but I don’t know what number that is. I’m just going to call it \(b\). So, \[
b = \log_{10} 2 \text{.}
\] This means that \(10^b = 2\). Now, I’m trying to find \(10^{\log_{10} 2}\). I’m just going to replace the exponent with \(b\): \[
10^{\log_{10} 2} = 10^b \text{.}
\] But I know that \(10^b = 2\)! So, \[
10^{\log_{10} 2} = 2 \text{.}
\]


2.2.2 A Special Base
You may have learned that \(\log\) without a base should be interpreted as log base 10. Unfortunately, this is not how statisticians do things. We use log with base \(e\) so often that for us, \(\log\) with no base means \(\log_e\). You may have learned that log with base \(e\) is the natural log, and is written \(\ln\). For whatever reason, statisticians use \(\log\) rather than \(\ln\).
Just to drive that point home, for a statistican, \(\log e = 1\), since \(e^1 = e\). In this textbook, if I want to use log with base 10, I’ll explicitly note it as \(\log_{10}\).


2.2.3 Properties
Similar to exponents, logarithms (or logs) have special properties. The properties hold for all bases, but for notational simplicity I’m just going to use log with base \(e\).
The first property is that \[
\log (ab) = \log a + \log b \text{.}
\]
The second property is that \[
a \log b = \log b^a \text{.}
\]
Note that, together, these imply that \[
\begin{align*}
\log \frac{a}{b} &= \log (a b^{-1}) \\
&= \log a + \log b^{-1} \\
&= \log a - \log b \text{.}
\end{align*}
\]


2.2.4 Arguments for Why the Properties Are True
If you need convincing that the properties are true, this section has arguments why.

2.2.4.1 Property 1
Suppose \[
\begin{align*}
\log(ab) &= x \\
\log a &= y \\
\log b &= z \text{.}
\end{align*}
\] This is equivalent to \[
\begin{align*}
e^x &= ab \\
e^y &= a \\
e^z &= b \text{.}
\end{align*}
\] So, we can say \[
\begin{align*}
e^x &= e^y e^z \\
e^x &= e^{y+z} \\
\log e^x &= \log e^{y+z} \\
x &= y + z \\
\log(ab) &= \log a + \log b \text{.}
\end{align*}
\]


2.2.4.2 Property 2
Define \[
\begin{align*}
a \log b &= x \\
\log b^a &= y \text{.}
\end{align*}
\] The first implies \[
\begin{align*}
\log b &= \frac{x}{a} \\
\exp\left(\frac{x}{a}\right) &= b \\
\end{align*}
\] and the second implies \(e^y = b^a\). We can combine these results to get \[
\begin{align*}
e^y &= \exp\left(\frac{x}{a}\right)^a \\
e^y &= \exp\left(a\frac{x}{a}\right) \\
e^y &= e^x \\
y &= x \\
\log b^a &= a \log b \text{.}
\end{align*}
\]

21.2 Lesson
Variance is a measure of the spread of a random variable, with higher variance meaning more spread. I’m going to show you the definition, but you don’t need to memorize it. We define variance as \[
Var(X) = E\left(\left(X - E(X)\right)^2\right) \text{.}
\] Variance is the expected squared deviation of a random variable from its mean. There are math reasons for using this particular method of measuring the spread of a random variable. I recognize that it might not be what you would choose if you wanted to communicate how spread out a distribution is.
\(Var(X)\) is a parameter. Sometimes we want to estimate it, making it an estimand. The most commonly used estimator of \(Var(X)\) is \[
\hat{\sigma}^2 = \frac{1}{n-1} \sum_{i=1}^n \left(X_i - \bar{X}\right)^2 \text{.}
\]
One issue with variance is that its units are the square of the original units of \(X_1,\dotsc,X_n\). If you’re studying heart rate in beats per minute, then the unit for the variance is squared beats per minute. This is usually not useful. So, we often work with the standard deviation, which is the square root of the variance. \[
\begin{align*}
SD(X) &= \sqrt{E\left(\left(X - E(X)\right)^2\right)} \\
\hat{\sigma} &= \sqrt{\frac{1}{n-1} \sum_{i=1}^n \left(X_i - \bar{X}\right)^2}
\end{align*}
\] The standard deviation has the same units as the original variable. Again, if you’re studying heart rate in beats per minute, the standard deviation is in beats per minute.
The standard error is the standard deviation of an estimator. I’m not entirely sure why it gets a special name, but I have a guess. Suppose your data are \(X_1,\dotsc,X_n\). We can use \(\sigma\) to represent the standard deviation of \(X_1\). If the data are iid, then \[
\begin{align*}
SD(X_1) &= \sigma \\
SD(\bar{X}) &= \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}} \text{.}
\end{align*}
\] The standard deviation of the data is different from the standard deviation of the sample average. So, we use standard deviation to clarify that we’re talking about the standard deviation of the data and standard error to talk about the standard deviation of the sample average.
Why do we care about this? “Standard error” often shows up in the output from statistical programming languages, so it’s important to know what it means.

66.2 Lesson
A Bernoulli random variable is a random variable that can only take on two possible values: 0 and 1. You may have heard this described as a binomial random variable with one trial, and that’s correct. The binomial distribution is an extension of the Bernoulli; if you added a bunch of independent Bernoulli random variables together, the result would follow a binomial distribution.
The Bernoulli distribution has one parameter: \(p\). It’s the probability that the Bernoulli random variable equals 1. To reiterate, if \(X\sim\text{Bernoulli}(p)\) then \[
\begin{align*}
P(X = 1) &= p \\
P(X = 0) &= 1 - p \text{.}
\end{align*}
\]
Let’s compute the mean: \[
\begin{align*}
E(X) &= \sum_{x=0}^1 x P(X = x) \\
&= 0 P(X = 0) + 1 P(X = 1) \\
&= P(X = 1) \\
&= p \text{.}
\end{align*}
\] So, the Bernoulli distribution’s sole parameter is also its expected value.
Now let’s compute the variance: \[
\begin{align*}
Var(X) &= E\left(\left(X - E(X)\right)^2\right) \\
&= \sum_{x=0}^1 \left(x - p\right)^2 P(X = x) \\
&= (0 - p)^2 P(X = 0) + (1 - p)^2 P(X = 1) \\
&= p^2 (1-p) + (1-p)^2 p \\
&= p(1-p)(p + 1-p) \\
&= p(1-p) \text{.}
\end{align*}
\]
Note that there’s a relationship between the mean and variance. Let’s plot the variance as a function of the mean.









This plot is telling us that the variability in the outcome will be highest when \(p = 0.5\) and lowest when \(p = 0\) or \(p = 1\). Does this make sense intuitively?
When \(p\) is near 0, the outcome will almost certainly be 0. If we have a sample of real data, almost all of the observations will be 0. So, the variability will be low. Similarly, if \(p\) is near 1, the outcome will almost certainly be 1. In a sample of real data, almost all the observations will be 1. So, again, the variability will be low. If \(p = 0.5\), each observation is equally likely to be 0 or 1. In a real data set, about half of the observations should be 0 and about half should be 1. This is the most possible variance you could have.
